241
00:18:23,281 --> 00:18:26,280
Whoever chose the location of Machu Picchu couldn't have

242
00:18:26,281 --> 00:18:31,280
picked a more awe-inspiring site, or a more challenging one.

243
00:18:31,281 --> 00:18:35,280
The city is precariously perched on a ridge between Huayna

244
00:18:35,281 --> 00:18:38,280
Picchu and Machu Picchu Mountains.

245
00:18:38,281 --> 00:18:42,280
It's a 1,600-foot sheer drop to the river below.

246
00:18:42,281 --> 00:18:48,820
Designing and building something of such magnitude at this remote location was a monumental

247
00:18:48,821 --> 00:18:50,126
undertaking.

248
00:18:50,127 --> 00:18:58,180
Thousands of workers labored to clear the site, lay foundations, construct

249
00:18:58,181 --> 00:19:07,000
terraces, and erect buildings. This is Machu Picchu, as the Incas would

250
00:19:07,001 --> 00:19:11,040
500 years ago when they arrived here from their imperial capital

251
00:19:11,140 --> 00:19:12,500
of Cusco.

252
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:14,540
There would have been thatched roofs on the buildings,

253
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:18,340
but otherwise, it would have looked just like this.

254
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:23,520
The city boasts more than 600 terraces,

255
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:34,160
over 170 buildings, thousands of steps,

256
00:19:34,161 --> 00:19:39,660
several temples, and 16 fountains.

257
00:19:43,500 --> 00:19:46,400
The Inca utilized hundreds of thousands of stones

258
00:19:46,401 --> 00:19:50,520
to build their city, some massive.

259
00:19:50,521 --> 00:19:52,920
Fortunately, they didn't have to go far

260
00:19:52,921 --> 00:19:56,020
to find construction materials.

261
00:19:56,021 --> 00:20:01,160
People often ask, how did the Incas get these stones here

262
00:20:01,161 --> 00:20:03,660
to build this city?

263
00:20:03,661 --> 00:20:05,260
And you're looking at the answer.

264
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:07,560
This is the main quarry of Machu Picchu,

265
00:20:07,660 --> 00:20:10,100
a massive jumble of white granite,

266
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:13,600
which they use to create this splendid city that you can see

267
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:15,600
behind us.

268
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:20,820
The quality of Inca stonework is legendary.

269
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:24,320
Important buildings exhibit a precision of stone fitting that

270
00:20:24,420 --> 00:20:28,120
almost defies explanation.

271
00:20:28,220 --> 00:20:32,060
After 500 years, a knife blade can't be inserted between the

272
00:20:32,061 --> 00:20:35,100
between the stones.

273
00:20:35,101 --> 00:20:39,560
This is the rear wall of the Temple of the Sun, probably

274
00:20:39,561 --> 00:20:41,840
the most important temple in Machu Picchu,

275
00:20:41,841 --> 00:20:44,960
and fittingly the finest wall of the whole site.

276
00:20:44,961 --> 00:20:48,340
An extraordinary example of Inca stonework,

277
00:20:48,341 --> 00:20:53,780
beautiful level courses, each tapering to a smaller height

278
00:20:53,781 --> 00:20:54,900
as they go to the top.

279
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:01,420
How did they achieve such precision?

280
00:21:01,421 --> 00:21:04,460
What sophisticated tools did they use?

281
00:21:04,461 --> 00:21:06,520
They didn't have steel chisels.

282
00:21:06,521 --> 00:21:08,860
They didn't even have iron chisels.

283
00:21:08,861 --> 00:21:13,200
Remarkably, without iron tools, the Inca used rock

284
00:21:13,201 --> 00:21:15,260
to carve rock.

285
00:21:15,261 --> 00:21:17,420
This is a hammer stone.

286
00:21:17,421 --> 00:21:21,120
And you look at it, and you say, well, that's just a river rock.

287
00:21:21,121 --> 00:21:22,660
Precisely.

288
00:21:22,661 --> 00:21:26,620
They used river rocks, the hardest stone they could find.

289
00:21:26,621 --> 00:21:31,620
Here's a stone you see that was being cut in the quarries.

290
00:21:31,621 --> 00:21:38,620
They used hammer stones, very hard river stones, to pound away the rock.

291
00:21:38,621 --> 00:21:42,620
Some of the stones that were worked at Machu Picchu were very, very large.

292
00:21:42,621 --> 00:21:47,620
And this is a very big hammer stone that must have been used to break off very big chunks of stone.

293
00:21:47,621 --> 00:21:50,620
It might even have been used by two people.

294
00:21:50,621 --> 00:21:53,620
This stone probably weighs 30 pounds.

295
00:21:53,621 --> 00:21:57,120
And it comes from the river, so it must have been hauled up

296
00:21:57,220 --> 00:22:00,620
on the back of somebody all the way up here to Machu Picchu.

297
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:05,960
Some of the largest stones used at Machu Picchu

298
00:22:06,060 --> 00:22:09,160
weigh more than 20 tons.

299
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:11,580
How were they moved?

300
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:14,780
Architect and ancient technology enthusiast Vince Lee

301
00:22:14,880 --> 00:22:16,280
believes he has an answer.

302
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:21,980
In 1998, Lee and several volunteers

303
00:22:21,981 --> 00:22:26,780
conducted an experiment with a 13-ton block of marble.

304
00:22:26,781 --> 00:22:32,280
The goal was to move the stone up a ramp with a 25% grade, then

305
00:22:32,281 --> 00:22:36,980
turn the stone 90 degrees into position, like the builders of

306
00:22:36,981 --> 00:22:41,280
Machu Picchu might have. But Lee wanted to do it without

307
00:22:41,281 --> 00:22:46,280
using ropes. Earlier tests proved very large

308
00:22:46,281 --> 00:22:49,980
rocks could be dragged into place if you had enough men and

309
00:22:49,981 --> 00:22:53,980
space for them to work. But in tight corners or confined

310
00:22:53,981 --> 00:22:58,980
areas, ropes just didn't work. Lee's theory involved placing

311
00:22:58,981 --> 00:23:01,980
the stone on a wooden sled, which would be moved over a

312
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:07,980
wooden track. Using log levers, the volunteers

313
00:23:07,981 --> 00:23:11,980
hoped to inch the sled forward. We need a practice.

314
00:23:11,981 --> 00:23:14,980
Move, move, move.

315
00:23:14,981 --> 00:23:18,980
With a heave and a groan, the rock lurched forward.

316
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:25,980
Without problem, the team moved the block up the incline.

317
00:23:25,981 --> 00:23:30,980
Even turning the stone at the top of the ramp proved easy.

318
00:23:30,981 --> 00:23:36,980
Lee believes the Inca used the same technique.

319
00:23:36,981 --> 00:23:39,980
The most important buildings relied on the precise fit of

320
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:42,980
the stone and their sheer weight to keep them in place.

321
00:23:42,981 --> 00:23:46,980
But there is evidence that less important buildings used mortar

322
00:23:46,981 --> 00:23:53,780
mortar made with a combination of clay, earth, and small stones.

323
00:23:53,781 --> 00:23:57,060
Beside the city's stonework, another feature that distinguishes

324
00:23:57,061 --> 00:24:02,020
Machu Picchu is the terrace system.

325
00:24:02,021 --> 00:24:05,160
The terraces served two purposes.

326
00:24:05,161 --> 00:24:09,200
They provided a place to grow crops and also kept the city

327
00:24:09,201 --> 00:24:13,200
from sliding off the mountain.

328
00:24:13,201 --> 00:24:17,100
This is a fairly typical terrace here at Machu Picchu.

329
00:24:17,101 --> 00:24:20,700
It's about seven feet high, about 10 feet wide,

330
00:24:20,701 --> 00:24:23,280
and there are hundreds of terraces like this one here.

331
00:24:23,281 --> 00:24:26,820
And these terraces are the secret of why this site

332
00:24:26,821 --> 00:24:29,220
remains so well-preserved and so intact,

333
00:24:29,221 --> 00:24:34,780
despite 400 years of neglect under the forest.

334
00:24:34,781 --> 00:24:38,160
The Inca built terraces to cope with the 79 inches

335
00:24:38,161 --> 00:24:40,360
of annual rainfall.

336
00:24:40,361 --> 00:24:43,200
Constructing a terrace is what's really important.

337
00:24:43,201 --> 00:24:49,440
The terra starts out with a coarse rock base,

338
00:24:49,441 --> 00:24:53,580
which provides good water flow and stability.

339
00:24:53,581 --> 00:24:57,880
On top of these stones then would be smaller stones,

340
00:24:57,881 --> 00:25:02,820
and then a gravel layer, and then a sand layer.

341
00:25:02,821 --> 00:25:05,480
And then on top of that would be topsoil.

342
00:25:05,481 --> 00:25:09,360
And the topsoil is oftentimes three feet thick,

343
00:25:09,361 --> 00:25:12,200
rich topsoil that has been brought in

344
00:25:12,201 --> 00:25:18,200
artificially placed to form the terrace for crops.

345
00:25:18,201 --> 00:25:21,200
Topsoil was most likely brought to the site from the flood

346
00:25:21,201 --> 00:25:28,200
plain 1,600 feet below, basket by basket.

347
00:25:28,201 --> 00:25:32,200
Stratified construction allowed rainwater to gently percolate

348
00:25:32,201 --> 00:25:39,200
down through the terraces. Until recently, scientists only

349
00:25:39,201 --> 00:25:42,200
speculated as to what crops might have been raised to feed the

350
00:25:42,201 --> 00:25:46,840
population. But in the mid-1990s, soil samples

351
00:25:46,841 --> 00:25:53,560
proved maize was the primary crop grown at Machu Picchu, along with potatoes and even avocados.

352
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:04,360
In total, Machu Picchu had 12 acres of terraced land within its walls on which to grow crops.

353
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:10,120
Knowing the type of food grown and the amount of land available led to an interesting discovery.

354
00:26:10,121 --> 00:26:15,120
What we concluded was that the terraces of Machu Picchu

355
00:26:15,220 --> 00:26:19,120
could not have supplied more than 55 people

356
00:26:19,220 --> 00:26:22,060
with appropriate nutrients.

357
00:26:22,160 --> 00:26:26,060
That meant that food had to be imported.

358
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:31,080
Scientists estimate Machu Picchu had 300 to 750 residents.

359
00:26:31,180 --> 00:26:33,380
So where did the food come from?

360
00:26:33,480 --> 00:26:38,380
There's the great Urubamba River floodplain 1,600 feet below,

361
00:26:38,381 --> 00:26:42,380
below, and we know that there was farming down below because

362
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:47,920
of the wide floodplain. The terraces served

363
00:26:48,020 --> 00:26:51,920
Machu Picchu better as claws, helping the city grip onto the

364
00:26:52,020 --> 00:26:54,760
mountain. Without them, the buildings

365
00:26:54,860 --> 00:26:58,940
would have tumbled down the mountain long ago.

366
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:02,380
The enormous construction effort exemplifies just how important

367
00:27:02,480 --> 00:27:07,740
the person was who built it. But who had the resources?

368
00:27:07,741 --> 00:27:12,840
who built Machu Picchu. At Machu Picchu, interior room

369
00:27:12,841 --> 00:27:16,640
walls and floors were often covered with plaster made of

370
00:27:16,641 --> 00:27:23,640
clay and lime. From its foundations to fountains,

371
00:27:23,641 --> 00:27:28,640
terraces to temples, Machu Picchu was a spectacular engineering

372
00:27:28,641 --> 00:27:32,180
achievement. But before construction could even

373
00:27:32,181 --> 00:27:38,980
begin. Someone had to design it. The architects may be unknown, but

374
00:27:38,981 --> 00:27:45,780
their plan is revealing its secrets. The city is divided into an agricultural

375
00:27:45,781 --> 00:27:51,620
sector and an urban sector. The urban sector, which covers five

376
00:27:51,621 --> 00:27:56,820
acres, is further split in two, separated by a large plaza.

377
00:27:56,821 --> 00:28:01,820
Now, at Machu Picchu, the western sector is the major

378
00:28:01,821 --> 00:28:04,820
temples and the Incas' residence.

379
00:28:04,821 --> 00:28:10,820
The eastern sector was for the visiting priests and family,

380
00:28:10,821 --> 00:28:13,820
but it also had the workshops.

381
00:28:13,821 --> 00:28:16,820
Incan architects designed Machu Picchu to exist

382
00:28:16,821 --> 00:28:20,820
symbiotically with nature, and many features accentuate

383
00:28:20,821 --> 00:28:22,820
the landscape.

384
00:28:22,821 --> 00:28:27,060
This is the main Inca gateway as you come into the city of

385
00:28:27,160 --> 00:28:28,220
Machu Picchu.

386
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:31,820
And as you can see, it perfectly frames the mountain of

387
00:28:31,920 --> 00:28:33,560
Huayna Picchu behind us.

388
00:28:33,660 --> 00:28:35,760
That's not an accident.

389
00:28:35,860 --> 00:28:38,360
At Machu Picchu, nature spoke,

390
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:40,980
and the Inca listened.

391
00:28:41,080 --> 00:28:42,480
The Incas worship nature.

392
00:28:42,580 --> 00:28:47,380
They worship what they called huaycas, rocks, streams,

393
00:28:47,480 --> 00:28:48,920
springs.

394
00:28:48,921 --> 00:28:52,260
the earth was filled with sacred power.

395
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:56,860
So you can imagine that at Machu Picchu,

396
00:28:56,960 --> 00:28:59,560
they found a place which was particularly potent

397
00:28:59,660 --> 00:29:00,960
with sacred power.

398
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:06,100
And so the whole area was a huaca, if you like.

399
00:29:06,200 --> 00:29:08,580
To the Incas, there was little distinction

400
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:11,100
between religion and science.

401
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:13,620
Because they left no written records,

402
00:29:13,720 --> 00:29:16,240
we don't know exactly what the Incas intentions were

403
00:29:16,241 --> 00:29:19,740
were for specific buildings.

404
00:29:19,741 --> 00:29:22,740
But scientists speculate several buildings served both a

405
00:29:22,741 --> 00:29:27,280
religious function and a utilitarian purpose.

406
00:29:27,281 --> 00:29:30,280
The one that the tourists are all brought to first is the

407
00:29:30,281 --> 00:29:34,400
Temple of the Sun. It's a very special place because it was

408
00:29:34,401 --> 00:29:38,900
built around and on top of a very huge rock.

409
00:29:38,901 --> 00:29:43,900
This building, which is circular, surrounds the rock

410
00:29:43,901 --> 00:29:46,900
and protects it because the rock is what's sacred.

411
00:29:46,901 --> 00:29:49,900
The rock is a huaca or a shrine.

412
00:29:49,901 --> 00:29:53,900
And this particular huaca has an eastern window.

413
00:29:53,901 --> 00:29:58,900
And on a certain day of the year, the winter solstice,

414
00:29:58,901 --> 00:30:00,900
the sun comes through that window,

415
00:30:00,901 --> 00:30:04,900
and the stone or the huaca is cut in a certain way

416
00:30:04,901 --> 00:30:07,900
so that the sunlight coming through that window

417
00:30:07,901 --> 00:30:09,900
is split in half.

418
00:30:09,901 --> 00:30:12,900
So it's a solar observatory.

419
00:30:13,900 --> 00:30:16,240
By knowing the position of the sun,

420
00:30:16,241 --> 00:30:21,100
the Incas could determine when to plant crops.

421
00:30:21,101 --> 00:30:24,640
Perhaps another temple ensured the sun wouldn't slip away

422
00:30:24,641 --> 00:30:28,880
entirely beyond the horizon, never to rise again.

423
00:30:28,881 --> 00:30:31,280
Well, this is the famous intiwatana,

424
00:30:31,281 --> 00:30:33,940
the hitching post of the sun.

425
00:30:33,941 --> 00:30:36,400
That word is a modern name that's

426
00:30:36,401 --> 00:30:39,860
been invented to describe this kind of rock.

427
00:30:39,861 --> 00:30:43,580
It's believed that every major Inca site had one of these,

428
00:30:43,581 --> 00:30:44,980
But none of them survived.

429
00:30:44,981 --> 00:30:47,480
They were destroyed by the Spanish invaders.

430
00:30:47,481 --> 00:30:52,120
They speculated that the Incas performed a ceremony here

431
00:30:52,121 --> 00:30:56,460
where they ritually tied the sun to this post,

432
00:30:56,461 --> 00:30:59,820
prevented from moving any further along the horizon

433
00:30:59,821 --> 00:31:01,800
at the time of the winter solstice.

434
00:31:01,801 --> 00:31:03,560
But of course, this is still speculation.

435
00:31:03,561 --> 00:31:06,600
We don't know really what this stone was used for.

436
00:31:06,601 --> 00:31:11,380
And it's another of Machu Picchu's mysteries.

437
00:31:11,381 --> 00:31:15,780
revered the mountains and the spirits inhabiting them.

438
00:31:15,880 --> 00:31:17,380
They even carved them in effigy.

439
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:24,420
We call this area here the sacred plaza.

440
00:31:24,520 --> 00:31:28,460
It's characterized by this tremendous rock behind me,

441
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:33,160
which has been carved to profile the horizon behind it

442
00:31:33,260 --> 00:31:34,540
and the mountain.

443
00:31:34,640 --> 00:31:39,640
And I think this is the kind of place where speakers may have

444
00:31:39,641 --> 00:31:43,640
have addressed a gathered assembly.

445
00:31:43,641 --> 00:31:47,600
Another gathering place was the open central plaza,

446
00:31:47,601 --> 00:31:52,180
which separates the eastern and western urban sections.

447
00:31:52,181 --> 00:31:54,600
This is a very cramped environment at Mechapigi.

448
00:31:54,601 --> 00:31:56,040
We're on the top of a mountain ridge.

449
00:31:56,041 --> 00:31:57,900
There's not much space here.

450
00:31:57,901 --> 00:32:02,640
So they managed to incorporate a fairly large chunk of ground

451
00:32:02,641 --> 00:32:05,540
where people could walk around in.

452
00:32:05,541 --> 00:32:08,140
The open plaza, the grand temples,

453
00:32:08,141 --> 00:32:13,440
stonework made Machu Picchu a city fit for a king.

454
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:16,880
And maybe that's exactly who it was for.

455
00:32:16,920 --> 00:32:21,180
One collection of impressive buildings might offer a hint.

456
00:32:21,220 --> 00:32:27,400
Hiram Bingham christened them the King's Group.

457
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:32,400
So here we are in the King's Group, the Royal Palace.

458
00:32:32,440 --> 00:32:36,040
Beautiful stonework. As you can see, this is one of the

459
00:32:36,041 --> 00:32:40,140
that drew Hiram Bingham to the theory of the King's Group or

460
00:32:40,141 --> 00:32:44,240
Royal Palace is these big, big massive lintels, which are the

461
00:32:44,241 --> 00:32:49,280
biggest of any residential kind of building in Machu Picchu.

462
00:32:49,281 --> 00:32:53,000
Each lintel weighs about three tons, and the Inca raised

463
00:32:53,001 --> 00:32:57,600
them without a crane. The building is also next to

464
00:32:57,601 --> 00:33:00,800
Fountain Number One. The resident of the King's Group

465
00:33:00,801 --> 00:33:04,440
had first access to the water, indicating this person was

466
00:33:04,441 --> 00:33:09,380
was important. The water delivery and drainage

467
00:33:09,381 --> 00:33:12,540
system is one of the most impressive engineering works at

468
00:33:12,541 --> 00:33:17,680
Machu Picchu. Inca designers rooted water from

469
00:33:17,681 --> 00:33:22,100
a spring a half mile away into the heart of the city and

470
00:33:22,101 --> 00:33:27,300
delivered it to a series of 16 fountains.

471
00:33:27,301 --> 00:33:30,400
The rushing water mimicked the roar of the sacred Urubamba

472
00:33:30,401 --> 00:33:34,000
River below. Without water, it wouldn't

473
00:33:34,001 --> 00:33:36,300
built. It was absolutely essential.

474
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:40,400
The planning started with development of the spring on

475
00:33:40,500 --> 00:33:44,140
the north side of Machu Picchu Mountain.

476
00:33:44,240 --> 00:33:50,520
From the spring, a 2,450-foot long stone-lined canal

477
00:33:50,620 --> 00:33:54,160
carried drinking water to the fountains.

478
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:59,500
The location of the spring, coupled with the law of

479
00:33:59,501 --> 00:34:03,740
The law of physics governing the slope at which water runs

480
00:34:03,741 --> 00:34:09,740
appropriately in a small canal meant that the site of Fountain

481
00:34:09,741 --> 00:34:13,740
Number One was predetermined by nature.

482
00:34:13,741 --> 00:34:16,740
Two of the most important buildings, the Temple of the

483
00:34:16,741 --> 00:34:20,100
Sun and the Royal Residence, were built adjacent to Fountain

484
00:34:20,101 --> 00:34:23,740
Number One. Therefore, the layout of the city

485
00:34:23,741 --> 00:34:26,660
may have been partially determined by the location

486
00:34:26,661 --> 00:34:31,660
the spring. The sophisticated hydraulic system

487
00:34:31,661 --> 00:34:35,660
is not only a testament to the genius of Inca engineers.

488
00:34:35,661 --> 00:34:40,660
It also helps answer questions about the people who lived there.

489
00:34:40,661 --> 00:34:45,660
In the late 1980s, after 80 years of speculation, the

490
00:34:45,661 --> 00:34:51,660
mysteries of Machu Picchu were about to be revealed.

491
00:34:51,661 --> 00:34:54,660
An estimated 60% of the construction effort at Machu

492
00:34:54,661 --> 00:34:59,420
of Machu Picchu is unseen, lying below ground in the form of site

493
00:34:59,421 --> 00:35:06,500
preparation, subsurface drainage, and foundations.

494
00:35:06,501 --> 00:35:11,300
Since Hiram Bingham discovered Machu Picchu in 1911, the site

495
00:35:11,301 --> 00:35:15,660
has been shrouded in mystery. But Bingham had several theories

496
00:35:15,661 --> 00:35:20,020
which endured for decades. Was Machu Picchu the birthplace

497
00:35:20,021 --> 00:35:24,420
the race of the Inca? Or Vilcabamba, the last Incan

498
00:35:24,421 --> 00:35:28,420
refuge from the Spanish? Or could it have been a sacred

499
00:35:28,421 --> 00:35:34,660
center for the virgins of the sun? In the last 20 years, a

500
00:35:34,661 --> 00:35:39,380
dedicated group of scientists have labored to find answers.

501
00:35:39,381 --> 00:35:43,180
Today, the cloud of mystery surrounding Machu Picchu is

502
00:35:43,181 --> 00:35:49,220
beginning to dissipate. In 1983, Richard Berger and

503
00:35:49,221 --> 00:35:54,820
Salazar, anthropologist at Yale University, started re-examining the artifacts of the

504
00:35:54,821 --> 00:35:56,791
Hiram Bingham expeditions.

505
00:35:56,792 --> 00:36:02,020
The artifacts, most of which had been locked away for 80 years in the

506
00:36:02,021 --> 00:36:07,860
basement of the Peabody Museum of Natural History at Yale University, began to reveal surprising

507
00:36:07,861 --> 00:36:10,596
information about Machu Picchu.

508
00:36:10,597 --> 00:36:16,420
For example, here what you see is a jar with two vertical handles.

509
00:36:16,421 --> 00:36:22,460
This jar particularly belongs to a group named Coyas.

510
00:36:22,920 --> 00:36:29,680
This ethnic group was conquered by the Incas and brought to Machu Picchu.

511
00:36:30,320 --> 00:36:33,840
They were very famous for the masonry.

512
00:36:34,360 --> 00:36:39,180
Machu Picchu probably was made by the Coyas.

513
00:36:39,920 --> 00:36:44,000
The Coyas predated the Incas and were superior builders.

514
00:36:44,001 --> 00:36:48,000
Before being conquered, they controlled Lake Titicaca

515
00:36:48,001 --> 00:36:51,000
on the Peruvian-Bolivian border.

516
00:36:51,001 --> 00:36:55,000
Looking at the materials, the artifacts, the tools,

517
00:36:55,001 --> 00:37:00,000
we came to the conclusion that these people were very specialized

518
00:37:00,001 --> 00:37:04,000
craftsmen from different parts of the empire.

519
00:37:04,001 --> 00:37:07,000
Had the Coyas built the city to be the last holdout

520
00:37:07,001 --> 00:37:09,000
of the Inca?

521
00:37:09,001 --> 00:37:13,000
The fabled Vilcabamba?

522
00:37:13,001 --> 00:37:20,360
In the early 1960s, American adventurer Gene Savoy answered with a resounding, no.

523
00:37:20,900 --> 00:37:34,034
Savoy correctly identified the real Vilcabamba at a place called Espiritu Pampa, or Plain of the Ghosts, 50 miles northwest of Machu Picchu, while retracing Bingham's

524
00:37:34,035 --> 00:37:35,300
1911 expedition.

525
00:37:37,140 --> 00:37:40,640
Ironically, Bingham had found Vilcabamba in 1911.

526
00:37:41,480 --> 00:37:42,560
He just didn't know it.

527
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:47,000
The site Bingham discovered was so overgrown, he couldn't see

528
00:37:47,001 --> 00:37:50,000
the hundreds of buildings the forest concealed.

529
00:37:50,001 --> 00:37:56,000
Furthermore, reexamining the artifacts indicated construction

530
00:37:56,001 --> 00:38:01,000
of Machu Picchu probably began around 1450, 80 years before the

531
00:38:01,001 --> 00:38:06,000
conquest. Then did the Coyas build

532
00:38:06,001 --> 00:38:10,000
Machu Picchu to be a sacred center for the Virgins of the

533
00:38:10,001 --> 00:38:16,500
With modern forensic tools and 20 years of practical experience,

534
00:38:16,501 --> 00:38:20,640
forensic anthropologist John Verano put the myth to rest

535
00:38:20,641 --> 00:38:25,240
when he reevaluated the skeletons discovered by Bingham.

536
00:38:25,241 --> 00:38:29,060
They found out that, in fact, there were men and women,

537
00:38:29,061 --> 00:38:31,560
that many of the women had born children,

538
00:38:31,561 --> 00:38:35,060
and that myth just didn't fit anymore.

539
00:38:35,061 --> 00:38:38,400
So what was Machu Picchu?

540
00:38:38,401 --> 00:38:44,400
For centuries, the answer lay buried in a Cusco archive.

541
00:38:44,401 --> 00:38:48,400
In the late 1980s, Berkeley anthropologist John Rowe was

542
00:38:48,401 --> 00:38:52,400
analyzing 16th-century records when he discovered a group of

543
00:38:52,401 --> 00:38:56,400
legal documents that made reference to a royal retreat

544
00:38:56,401 --> 00:39:01,400
called Pichu. Relating it to known sites,

545
00:39:01,401 --> 00:39:06,400
the retreat called Pichu looked to be referring to Machu

546
00:39:06,401 --> 00:39:09,900
In this document, it says that those lands,

547
00:39:09,901 --> 00:39:12,900
lands around this place called Picchu,

548
00:39:12,901 --> 00:39:15,900
belong to the lineage of Pachacuti,

549
00:39:15,901 --> 00:39:18,400
the greatest of all the Inca emperors.

550
00:39:18,401 --> 00:39:23,400
These were documents that had been generated by a legal case

551
00:39:23,401 --> 00:39:27,400
in which descendants of Pachacuti were asking

552
00:39:27,401 --> 00:39:31,400
for the return of lands that belonged to them.

553
00:39:31,401 --> 00:39:34,400
The theory made sense.

554
00:39:34,401 --> 00:39:38,400
A royal retreat built for Pachacuti.

555
00:39:38,401 --> 00:39:43,400
New evidence suggested construction began during his reign.

556
00:39:43,401 --> 00:39:46,400
Pachacuti really created the Inca Empire.

557
00:39:46,401 --> 00:39:50,400
He had the power necessary to bring the most talented people

558
00:39:50,401 --> 00:39:53,400
of the time and basically unlimited labor,

559
00:39:53,401 --> 00:39:58,400
and then create a masterpiece to sculpt a mountain

560
00:39:58,401 --> 00:40:00,400
and produce some of the finest architecture

561
00:40:00,401 --> 00:40:03,400
that the world has ever seen.

562
00:40:03,401 --> 00:40:07,640
The legal documents and other facts helped convince Rome

563
00:40:07,641 --> 00:40:13,000
Machu Picchu was a retreat or country palace.

564
00:40:13,001 --> 00:40:16,940
The site was a few days' walk from Cusco, and emperors were

565
00:40:16,941 --> 00:40:22,000
known to own royal estates. At an elevation of 8,000 feet,

566
00:40:22,001 --> 00:40:25,500
Machu Picchu is definitely a city in the clouds.

567
00:40:25,501 --> 00:40:30,300
But surprisingly, it lies 3,000 feet lower than Cusco, which is

568
00:40:30,301 --> 00:40:35,000
11,000 feet in elevation. Pachacuti could have escaped the

569
00:40:35,001 --> 00:40:39,440
cold winters of Cusco by traveling to Machu Picchu, where it would have been warmer.

570
00:40:43,340 --> 00:40:48,680
The new description of Machu Picchu as a royal retreat doesn't diminish its mystical qualities.

571
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:58,280
I believe that Machu Picchu was essentially a sacred religious site that the Incas constructed

572
00:40:58,281 --> 00:41:01,040
because of this location.

573
00:41:01,320 --> 00:41:04,800
We have to remember Pachacuti was a religious, spiritual leader,

574
00:41:04,920 --> 00:41:06,320
not only a political leader.

575
00:41:06,860 --> 00:41:13,240
So it fits with the concept of this being a private estate of Pachacuti

576
00:41:13,241 --> 00:41:16,980
that it was also an important sacred center.

577
00:41:18,140 --> 00:41:23,640
The general consensus today is Machu Picchu likely had a population of 300,

578
00:41:23,641 --> 00:41:28,140
swelling to 750 if the emperor and his entourage were in

579
00:41:28,141 --> 00:41:29,640
residence.

580
00:41:29,641 --> 00:41:34,980
The site was inhabited from 1450 to 1540 and ultimately

581
00:41:34,981 --> 00:41:36,880
abandoned.

582
00:41:36,881 --> 00:41:40,020
Perhaps it was the Spanish conquest itself that caused

583
00:41:40,021 --> 00:41:42,520
people to leave this place.

584
00:41:42,521 --> 00:41:46,020
When the Spanish conquered Cusco, the people living here at

585
00:41:46,021 --> 00:41:49,600
that time would have been Pachacuti's kin group, what they

586
00:41:49,601 --> 00:41:51,060
call his panaca.

587
00:41:51,061 --> 00:41:56,400
So it could be that those few remaining people of his kin

588
00:41:56,500 --> 00:42:01,200
group felt too isolated here.

589
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:04,400
They didn't have the means to sustain themselves.

590
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:06,500
They left.

591
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:08,920
The craftsmen brought from all parts of the empire

592
00:42:09,020 --> 00:42:12,320
to build the city, probably packed up and went home as

593
00:42:12,420 --> 00:42:14,820
well.

594
00:42:14,920 --> 00:42:18,720
War, famine, and a smallpox epidemic

595
00:42:18,721 --> 00:42:23,120
wiped out more than 300,000 Incas, helping to erase

596
00:42:23,220 --> 00:42:25,420
the memory of Pachacuti's grand estate

597
00:42:25,520 --> 00:42:27,920
from the memories of the privileged few who

598
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:31,260
would have known of its existence.

599
00:42:31,360 --> 00:42:34,460
The jungle took care of the rest.

600
00:42:34,560 --> 00:42:37,180
In the cloud forest region, the vegetation

601
00:42:37,280 --> 00:42:39,180
grows extremely fast.

602
00:42:39,280 --> 00:42:41,280
The people who maintain Machu Picchu today

603
00:42:41,380 --> 00:42:45,580
have a constant battle to keep these terraces clear of vegetation.

604
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:48,720
Within a few years of this place being a place

605
00:42:48,721 --> 00:42:51,020
it would have been invisible. It would have been covered

606
00:42:51,021 --> 00:42:54,720
with trees. Today, the only thing

607
00:42:54,721 --> 00:43:00,260
covering the site are tourists. Each year, more than 300,000

608
00:43:00,261 --> 00:43:05,000
people come to experience the majesty of Machu Picchu.

609
00:43:05,001 --> 00:43:09,780
The awe people feel when they visit Machu Picchu is, in a

610
00:43:09,781 --> 00:43:14,820
sense, an appreciation of ancient, proving culture.

611
00:43:14,821 --> 00:43:18,020
Machu Picchu is one of the last surviving links to a great

612
00:43:18,021 --> 00:43:24,020
civilization. Its people reach out from the mist and point us

613
00:43:24,021 --> 00:43:27,020
toward a better understanding of who they were through the

614
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:32,020
remarkable technology. Machu Picchu is a symbol of an

615
00:43:32,021 --> 00:43:38,020
emperor's power and a sacred bridge between mankind and

616
00:43:38,021 --> 00:43:42,020
nature. Modern science and modern

617
00:43:42,021 --> 00:43:45,020
research has answered many of the questions that have puzzled

618
00:43:45,021 --> 00:43:47,580
that have puzzled people in the nearly 100 years

619
00:43:47,581 --> 00:43:50,020
since Machu Picchu was discovered.

620
00:43:50,021 --> 00:43:52,820
And yet, it remains a mysterious place.

621
00:43:52,821 --> 00:43:56,300
We still ask ourselves why the Incas were prepared

622
00:43:56,301 --> 00:43:59,620
to go to such lengths to build this city here

623
00:43:59,621 --> 00:44:02,080
in such a remote location.

624
00:44:02,081 --> 00:44:04,600
We don't know the answers to all of those questions.

625
00:44:05,460 --> 00:44:07,580
Machu Picchu still is something of a mystery

626
00:44:07,581 --> 00:44:09,120
and perhaps it always will be.

